\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{Problem 1: Triaxial stretch: area change and normals}{178}{problemenv.826}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {E}{\ignorespaces Why the restriction is $\lambda _{1}\lambda _{2}\lambda _{3}>0$ and not $\lambda _{A}>0$. Bottom, the reference cube with its right-handed frame. Middle, two of the $\lambda $'s negative: the frame is turned through $\pi $ about $\vek  {e}_{3}$ but is still right handed, the box product is still positive, and the deformation is a genuine one, a pure stretch followed by a half turn. Top, one $\lambda $ negative: the frame has become left handed, the box product is negative, and $\vek  {R}$ is a mirror. No continuous motion starting from $\vek  {F}=\vek  {1}$ can reach it, because $J$ would have to pass through zero and $\vek  {F}$ would cease to be invertible at that instant.}}{181}{figure.828}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:signs}{{E}{181}{Why the restriction is $\lambda _{1}\lambda _{2}\lambda _{3}>0$ and not $\lambda _{A}>0$. Bottom, the reference cube with its right-handed frame. Middle, two of the $\lambda $'s negative: the frame is turned through $\pi $ about $\be _{3}$ but is still right handed, the box product is still positive, and the deformation is a genuine one, a pure stretch followed by a half turn. Top, one $\lambda $ negative: the frame has become left handed, the box product is negative, and $\bR $ is a mirror. No continuous motion starting from $\bF =\shift $ can reach it, because $J$ would have to pass through zero and $\bF $ would cease to be invertible at that instant}{figure.828}{}}
