\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {G}{\ignorespaces Problem 6: flexure. The through-thickness coordinate $X_{1}$ becomes the radius, $r=f(X_{1})$, so lines $X_{1}=\text  {const}$ (solid) become concentric arcs; the along-length coordinate $X_{2}$ becomes the azimuth, $\theta =g(X_{2})$, so lines $X_{2}=\text  {const}$ (dashed) become radial rays. A block of thickness $T$ and length $L$ is wrapped into a wedge subtending $g(L)-g(0)$. Cross-sections stay plane and normal to the arcs, which is why $\{\vek  {e}_{r},\vek  {e}_{\theta },\vek  {e}_{3}\}$ and $\{\cc@accent {"705E}{\vek  {E}}_{A}\}$ pair off in $\vek  {F}$ with no shear between them.}}{190}{figure.835}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:flexure}{{G}{190}{Problem 6: flexure. The through-thickness coordinate $X_{1}$ becomes the radius, $r=f(X_{1})$, so lines $X_{1}=\text {const}$ (solid) become concentric arcs; the along-length coordinate $X_{2}$ becomes the azimuth, $\theta =g(X_{2})$, so lines $X_{2}=\text {const}$ (dashed) become radial rays. A block of thickness $T$ and length $L$ is wrapped into a wedge subtending $g(L)-g(0)$. Cross-sections stay plane and normal to the arcs, which is why $\{\ber ,\bet ,\be _{3}\}$ and $\{\hbE _{A}\}$ pair off in $\bF $ with no shear between them}{figure.835}{}}
