\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces The spinning disc. Every point of the body moves with $\vek  {v}(\vek  {y})-\vek  {v}(\vek  {x})=\omega \vek  {n}\times (\vek  {y}-\vek  {x})$, which is linear in $\vek  {y}-\vek  {x}$ with a skew coefficient. Hence $\vek  {D}=\vek  {O}$: the motion is rigid. The vorticity is $\vek  {w}=\omega \vek  {n}$, the angular velocity itself, because $\operatorname  {curl}\vek  {v}$ of a rigid field is twice the angular velocity and \eqr  {4.23} halves it.}}{221}{figure.919}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:disc}{{4.1}{221}{The spinning disc. Every point of the body moves with $\bv (\by )-\bv (\bx )=\omega \bn \times (\by -\bx )$, which is linear in $\by -\bx $ with a skew coefficient. Hence $\bD =\Ob $: the motion is rigid. The vorticity is $\bw =\omega \bn $, the angular velocity itself, because $\crl \bv $ of a rigid field is twice the angular velocity and \eqr {4.23} halves it}{figure.919}{}}
