\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {A}{\ignorespaces Why \eqr  {6.70} carries a term that $\vek  {f}=m\cc@accent {"707F}{\cc@accent {"7016}{\vek  {x}}}$ does not. The rotational momentum $\vek  {J}\vek  {\omega }$ is not parallel to $\vek  {\omega }$ unless $\vek  {\omega }$ lies along a principal axis: the inertia tensor weights the components of $\vek  {\omega }$ unequally, and tilts the result toward the axis of larger moment of inertia. The angle $\vartheta $ between them is what the gyroscopic term $\vek  {\omega }\times \vek  {J}\vek  {\omega }$ measures, and that term points out of the plane of the two vectors. The dashed curve is the section of $\vek  {u}\cdot \vek  {J}\vek  {u}=\text  {const}$, elongated along the axis of \emph  {smaller} inertia because the quadratic form is smaller there.}}{299}{figure.1226}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ch6inertia}{{A}{299}{Why \eqr {6.70} carries a term that $\vek {f}=m\ddot {\bar {\bx }}$ does not. The rotational momentum $\vek {J}\bom $ is not parallel to $\bom $ unless $\bom $ lies along a principal axis: the inertia tensor weights the components of $\bom $ unequally, and tilts the result toward the axis of larger moment of inertia. The angle $\vartheta $ between them is what the gyroscopic term $\bom \times \vek {J}\bom $ measures, and that term points out of the plane of the two vectors. The dashed curve is the section of $\ip \bu {\vek {J}\bu }=\text {const}$, elongated along the axis of \emph {smaller} inertia because the quadratic form is smaller there}{figure.1226}{}}
