\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces An arbitrary part of a body divided into two sub-parts by an interior surface $\sigma $. Euler's postulate holds for $P_{t1}$, for $P_{t2}$ and for $P_{t}$; the first two together must reproduce the third, and the difference is exactly \eqr  {6.80}. Shrinking $P_{t}$ onto $\sigma $ kills the volume terms and leaves Cauchy's lemma $\vek  {t}_{21}=-\vek  {t}_{12}$. Note that $\sigma $ belongs to $\partial P_{t1}$ and to $\partial P_{t2}$ but not to $\partial P_{t}$: that is the whole of the bookkeeping.}}{306}{figure.1252}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ch6cut}{{6.4}{306}{An arbitrary part of a body divided into two sub-parts by an interior surface $\sigma $. Euler's postulate holds for $P_{t1}$, for $P_{t2}$ and for $P_{t}$; the first two together must reproduce the third, and the difference is exactly \eqr {6.80}. Shrinking $P_{t}$ onto $\sigma $ kills the volume terms and leaves Cauchy's lemma $\bt _{21}=-\bt _{12}$. Note that $\sigma $ belongs to $\partial P_{t1}$ and to $\partial P_{t2}$ but not to $\partial P_{t}$: that is the whole of the bookkeeping}{figure.1252}{}}
