\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.11}{\ignorespaces Resolution of the traction into normal and shear components. By Noll's theorem the traction on $s$ at $\vek  {x}$ is the traction on the tangent plane there, and by Cauchy's theorem it is $\vek  {T}\vek  {n}$. Splitting it with the projection \eqr  {6.121} gives $\sigma \vek  {n}$ along the normal and $\tau \vek  {s}$ in the plane. The normal part $\sigma =\vek  {n}\cdot \vek  {T}\vek  {n}$ may have either sign; the shear part $\tau =|\mathbb  {P}_{(\vek  {n})}\vek  {T}\vek  {n}|$ cannot be negative, its sign having been absorbed into $\vek  {s}$. Planes on which $\tau =0$ are exactly the principal planes of $\vek  {T}$, and by Theorem~\ref {thm:spectral} three mutually perpendicular ones exist at every point.}}{332}{figure.1384}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ch6shear}{{6.11}{332}{Resolution of the traction into normal and shear components. By Noll's theorem the traction on $s$ at $\bx $ is the traction on the tangent plane there, and by Cauchy's theorem it is $\bT \bn $. Splitting it with the projection \eqr {6.121} gives $\sigma \bn $ along the normal and $\tau \bs $ in the plane. The normal part $\sigma =\ip \bn {\bT \bn }$ may have either sign; the shear part $\tau =|\Proj \bn \bT \bn |$ cannot be negative, its sign having been absorbed into $\bs $. Planes on which $\tau =0$ are exactly the principal planes of $\bT $, and by Theorem~\ref {thm:spectral} three mutually perpendicular ones exist at every point}{figure.1384}{}}
