\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces A body as perceived by two observers. The same two material points are seen at $\vek  {x},\vek  {y}$ by $O$ and at $\vek  {x}^{+},\vek  {y}^{+}$ by $O^{+}$, and the segment joining them has the same length $\ell $ in both pictures: that is \eqr  {7.2}, and it is the only geometric agreement assumed. The two observers need not agree about which direction the segment points, about where the origin is, or about anything else. Section~\ref {sec:ch7obs} shows that this single agreement forces the relation between the pictures to be \eqr  {7.3}, a uniform orthogonal $\vek  {Q}(t)$ followed by a translation $\vek  {c}(t)$, and that both may depend on time in any way whatever.}}{417}{figure.1571}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ch7two}{{7.1}{417}{A body as perceived by two observers. The same two material points are seen at $\bx ,\by $ by $O$ and at $\bx ^{+},\by ^{+}$ by $O^{+}$, and the segment joining them has the same length $\ell $ in both pictures: that is \eqr {7.2}, and it is the only geometric agreement assumed. The two observers need not agree about which direction the segment points, about where the origin is, or about anything else. Section~\ref {sec:ch7obs} shows that this single agreement forces the relation between the pictures to be \eqr {7.3}, a uniform orthogonal $\bQ (t)$ followed by a translation $\bc (t)$, and that both may depend on time in any way whatever}{figure.1571}{}}
