\newlabel{rem:ch7transport}{{7.11}{428}{\eqr {7.8} read as a decomposition, and a one-line route to \eqr {7.33}}{theorem.1594}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {B}{\ignorespaces Equation \eqr  {7.8} as a decomposition, drawn in the frame of $O^{+}$ with $\vek  {c}=\vek  {0}$ for clarity. In (a) the velocity $O^{+}$ records for one particle is the sum of the velocity $O$ records, carried over by $\vek  {Q}$, and the transport velocity $\vek  {v}^{+}_{\text  {tr}}$, which is what $O^{+}$ would record if the particle were nailed to $O$'s frame. Since the platform of $O^{+}$ turns at $\vek  {\omega }_{p}$, the transport velocity runs backwards, $\vek  {\Omega }\vek  {s}=-\vek  {\omega }_{p}\times \vek  {s}$, as Remark~\ref {rem:ch7omega} requires. In (b) the same field is drawn at twelve places: its arrows grow linearly with the distance from the axis and are everywhere tangential, which is to say that it is the velocity field of a rigid rotation, \eqr  {4.29}. That is the whole reason $\vek  {D}$ survives a change of observer and $\vek  {W}$ does not: the observer adds the gradient of the field in (b), and the field in (b) stretches nothing.}}{429}{figure.1595}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ch7transport}{{B}{429}{Equation \eqr {7.8} as a decomposition, drawn in the frame of $O^{+}$ with $\bc =\bze $ for clarity. In (a) the velocity $O^{+}$ records for one particle is the sum of the velocity $O$ records, carried over by $\bQ $, and the transport velocity $\bv ^{+}_{\text {tr}}$, which is what $O^{+}$ would record if the particle were nailed to $O$'s frame. Since the platform of $O^{+}$ turns at $\bom _{p}$, the transport velocity runs backwards, $\bOm \bs =-\bom _{p}\times \bs $, as Remark~\ref {rem:ch7omega} requires. In (b) the same field is drawn at twelve places: its arrows grow linearly with the distance from the axis and are everywhere tangential, which is to say that it is the velocity field of a rigid rotation, \eqr {4.29}. That is the whole reason $\bD $ survives a change of observer and $\bW $ does not: the observer adds the gradient of the field in (b), and the field in (b) stretches nothing}{figure.1595}{}}
\newlabel{eq:7.9}{{{7.9}}{429}{Transformation of the motion}{AMS.1597}{}}
\newlabel{rem:ch7fourterms}{{7.12}{430}{the four terms of \eqr {7.9}}{theorem.1598}{}}
\newlabel{eq:7.10}{{{7.10}}{430}{Transformation of the motion}{AMS.1601}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {C}{\ignorespaces The four terms of \eqr  {7.9}, drawn in the frame of $O^{+}$ as the four contributions they make to the inertial force \eqr  {7.63}. Each answers to one way in which the frame can move, and each has a signature that identifies it. (a) depends on neither the place nor the velocity of the particle, so it is indistinguishable from a uniform gravitational field, which is the content of Remark~\ref {rem:ch7inertial}. (b) is perpendicular to $\vek  {v}^{+}$, because $\vek  {\Omega }$ is skew, and therefore does no work: $\vek  {\Omega }\vek  {v}^{+}\cdot \vek  {v}^{+}=0$ by Lemma~\ref {lem:symskw}. It vanishes for a particle at rest in the frame. (c) points away from the axis with magnitude proportional to the distance from it, and is the only one of the four that a particle at rest in a steadily spinning frame feels. (d) appears only while the spin rate is changing and is azimuthal, in the sense opposite to the change. A frame may exhibit any combination of the four; the spinning planet of Problem 3 has (b) and (c) and nothing else.}}{431}{figure.1599}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ch7fourterms}{{C}{431}{The four terms of \eqr {7.9}, drawn in the frame of $O^{+}$ as the four contributions they make to the inertial force \eqr {7.63}. Each answers to one way in which the frame can move, and each has a signature that identifies it. (a) depends on neither the place nor the velocity of the particle, so it is indistinguishable from a uniform gravitational field, which is the content of Remark~\ref {rem:ch7inertial}. (b) is perpendicular to $\bv ^{+}$, because $\bOm $ is skew, and therefore does no work: $\ip {\bOm \bv ^{+}}{\bv ^{+}}=0$ by Lemma~\ref {lem:symskw}. It vanishes for a particle at rest in the frame. (c) points away from the axis with magnitude proportional to the distance from it, and is the only one of the four that a particle at rest in a steadily spinning frame feels. (d) appears only while the spin rate is changing and is azimuthal, in the sense opposite to the change. A frame may exhibit any combination of the four; the spinning planet of Problem 3 has (b) and (c) and nothing else}{figure.1599}{}}
