\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.2}{\ignorespaces Local change of reference. Only a neighbourhood of the one material point $p_{0}$ is relabelled, so $\vek  {\lambda }$ need not, and in general does not, extend to a map of the whole body; $\mu $ is a local reference configuration and nothing more. The two arrows on the right carry the same label because $\vek  {X}_{0}$ is a pivot: $p_{0}$ is assigned the same position vector by both placements, which is what allows the two response functions in \eqr  {8.13} to be compared at a single point. The pivot costs no generality, since translating $\mu $ leaves $\vek  {R}=\operatorname  {Grad}\vek  {\lambda }$ untouched.}}{499}{figure.1792}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ch8local}{{8.2}{499}{Local change of reference. Only a neighbourhood of the one material point $p_{0}$ is relabelled, so $\vek {\lambda }$ need not, and in general does not, extend to a map of the whole body; $\mu $ is a local reference configuration and nothing more. The two arrows on the right carry the same label because $\bX _{0}$ is a pivot: $p_{0}$ is assigned the same position vector by both placements, which is what allows the two response functions in \eqr {8.13} to be compared at a single point. The pivot costs no generality, since translating $\mu $ leaves $\bR =\Grad \vek {\lambda }$ untouched}{figure.1792}{}}
