\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {B}{\ignorespaces What Part 2 proves. The stretching $\vek  {D}$ has principal axes $\{\vek  {\nu }_{i}\}$ and principal values $\eta _{i}$, which say at what rate material fibres along those axes are lengthening or shortening. Equation \eqr  {9.29} says that the stress $\vek  {G}(\vek  {D})$ has \emph  {the same} axes, with new principal values $\beta _{i}$. Mechanically this means that a plane whose normal is a principal direction of stretching carries a purely normal traction: no shear stress acts on it. The argument for this is \eqr  {9.27}: a $180^{\circ }$ turn about $\vek  {\nu }_{1}$ leaves $\vek  {D}$ untouched, so by \eqr  {9.23} it must leave $\vek  {G}(\vek  {D})$ untouched, and the only directions a rotation fixes are along its own axis. Nothing about the material has been used, only the requirement that two observers agree.}}{548}{figure.1945}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ch9coaxial}{{B}{548}{What Part 2 proves. The stretching $\bD $ has principal axes $\{\bnu _{i}\}$ and principal values $\eta _{i}$, which say at what rate material fibres along those axes are lengthening or shortening. Equation \eqr {9.29} says that the stress $\bG (\bD )$ has \emph {the same} axes, with new principal values $\beta _{i}$. Mechanically this means that a plane whose normal is a principal direction of stretching carries a purely normal traction: no shear stress acts on it. The argument for this is \eqr {9.27}: a $180^{\circ }$ turn about $\bnu _{1}$ leaves $\bD $ untouched, so by \eqr {9.23} it must leave $\bG (\bD )$ untouched, and the only directions a rotation fixes are along its own axis. Nothing about the material has been used, only the requirement that two observers agree}{figure.1945}{}}
