\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {A}{\ignorespaces The normal stress effect of \eqr  {11.42}, with the undeformed block dashed. Sliding the top face of a block over the bottom one is not achieved by shear tractions alone. A uniform isotropic solid held in simple shear also pushes on the faces normal to $\vek  {e}_{1}$ and $\vek  {e}_{2}$, and the difference of those two normal stresses is $\gamma T_{12}$, whatever the material. Left is what the linear theory of Chapter 12 predicts, right what \eqr  {11.34} and \eqr  {11.36} require; the difference is of order $\gamma ^{2}$ and vanishes with the approximation.}}{657}{figure.2301}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ch11normal}{{A}{657}{The normal stress effect of \eqr {11.42}, with the undeformed block dashed. Sliding the top face of a block over the bottom one is not achieved by shear tractions alone. A uniform isotropic solid held in simple shear also pushes on the faces normal to $\be _{1}$ and $\be _{2}$, and the difference of those two normal stresses is $\gamma T_{12}$, whatever the material. Left is what the linear theory of Chapter 12 predicts, right what \eqr {11.34} and \eqr {11.36} require; the difference is of order $\gamma ^{2}$ and vanishes with the approximation}{figure.2301}{}}
